<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Nemrut</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nemrut Dağı</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Nemrut is a 2,134-metre mountain in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, near Adıyaman, crowned by the tomb sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-i-theos-of-commagene">Antiochus I Theos of Commagene</a>, built in the 1st century BCE. Colossal seated statues of the king and of gods combining Greek and Persian features flank an artificial mound of stone chips. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut.jpg" alt="Mount Nemrut" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klearchos Kapoutsis from Santorini, Greece · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Nemrut_-_East_Terrace_(4961323529).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
